                ),
                q4Title = "Computers in Children's Education: Boon or Bane",
                q4Prompt = "Some people consider that computers are more a hindrance than a help in children's education. Others say they have more advantages. Discuss both views and give your opinion. (Write at least 250 words)",
                q4Essay = "The ubiquity of personal computers and digital tablets in classrooms has ignited fervent dispute among educators and child psychologists. While critics caution that digital hardware cultivates chronic distraction, passive cognition, and physical lethargy, proponents maintain that digital tools provide unprecedented educational access. This essay evaluates both arguments before concluding that when guided by disciplined pedagogical oversight, the educational advantages of computers far surpass their drawbacks.\n\nOn the one hand, detractors rightly highlight the risks of unmonitored digital immersion. Computers are gateways to infinite non-educational distractions, including gamified algorithms, video streaming, and social media. When pupils rely excessively on internet search engines for instantaneous answers, they bypass the laborious cognitive struggle essential for memorization, deep textual analysis, and conceptual deduction. Furthermore, hours spent hunched over backlit screens contribute to ocular fatigue, sedentary obesity, and reduced handwriting dexterity.\n\nOn the other hand, dismissing computers as educational hindrances ignores their immense pedagogical utility. Digital devices democratize access to world-class educational repositories, interactive scientific simulations, and customized learning algorithms that adapt to each child's learning pace. For instance, a child studying astrophysics can interact with 3D planetary simulations that bring abstract textbook diagrams to life. Moreover, basic digital literacy and programming competencies are foundational prerequisites for twentieth-first-century employment; withholding computers from students leaves them severely handicapped in modern knowledge economies.\n\nIn conclusion, while unguided screen time can hinder attention spans, the pedagogical dividends of computers are indisputable. Educators must not ban technology, but rather institute structured digital hygiene, ensuring computers serve as powerful catalysts for intellectual inquiry.",
