                q1Title = "Tourist Footfall & Preservation Expenditure at Heritage Sites",
                q1Prompt = "The table below details annual tourist footfall (in millions) and associated preservation expenditure (in millions of euros) across four iconic World Heritage sites.\n\nSummarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant. (Write at least 150 words)",
                q1Report = "The comparative table details tourist attendance figures alongside associated conservation allocations across four world-renowned historical landmarks over a twenty-year timeframe.\n\nOverall, visitor numbers rose across all four heritage locations over the recorded period, with Mediterranean ruins sustaining the heaviest demographic volume. In contrast, while conservation funding increased universally, its growth pace lagged substantially behind tourist expansion rates.\n\nInitially, Mediterranean ruins welcomed 4.2 million visitors with an annual maintenance outlay of €18 million. By the end of the survey, annual arrivals surged to 8.6 million—more than doubling the initial total—while conservation budgets only climbed modestly to €26 million, revealing growing fiscal strain on site conservation.\n\nSimilarly, Asian historic temples witnessed a remarkable surge in footfall, ascending from 1.8 million to 5.4 million travelers, accompanied by a threefold escalation in preservation grants from €6 million to €19 million. Alpine fortresses and South American citadel complexes observed consistent, controlled growth, with attendance reaching 2.9 million and 2.1 million respectively, maintaining proportional conservation funding parity throughout.",
                q1Vocab = listOf(
                    Band9Vocabulary("Lagged substantially behind", "Collocation", "Grew much slower than", "Public infrastructure expenditure lagged substantially behind rapid urban population growth."),
                    Band9Vocabulary("Fiscal strain on conservation", "Formal Lexis", "Financial pressure on preservation", "Overtourism imposes acute fiscal strain on conservation authorities worldwide."),
                    Band9Vocabulary("Threefold escalation", "Data Expression", "Increased three times", "Preservation subsidies registered a threefold escalation over the course of two decades."),
                    Band9Vocabulary("Maintained funding parity", "Collocation", "Kept equal balanced resources", "Targeted endowments maintained funding parity throughout economic slowdowns.")
                ),
                q2Title = "The Online Shopping Revolution: Demise of High Street Retail",
                q2Prompt = "In some countries, an increasing number of people are using the internet to buy everyday items. What are the reasons for this? Is this a positive or negative development? (Write at least 250 words)",
                q2Essay = "The explosive ascendance of e-commerce has fundamentally revolutionized contemporary retail habits, with millions of households routinely ordering daily groceries, clothing, and household goods online. This seismic shift is fueled by unprecedented convenience and price competitiveness. In my view, while this evolution presents logistical and economic challenges for brick-and-mortar storefronts, it constitutes an overwhelmingly positive advancement for consumer welfare and commercial efficiency.\n\nThe principal catalysts behind the boom in online retail are supreme convenience and expansive consumer choice. Traditional shopping requires consumers to endure congested traffic, navigate crowded aisles, and queue at payment counters. In sharp contrast, e-commerce platforms enable consumers to compare hundreds of vendors, inspect verified customer reviews, and complete purchases within seconds from their smartphones, with goods delivered directly to their doorstep. Furthermore, digital marketplaces bypass the exorbitant overhead expenses of physical rent and extensive retail staffing, allowing them to pass substantial cost savings directly to consumers.\n\nFrom a societal standpoint, this retail revolution delivers immense dividends. For mobility-impaired individuals, working parents, and remote rural communities, direct delivery of groceries and pharmaceuticals is life-changing. Although critics lament that online shopping causes the decline of traditional high street retail and increases packaging waste, it also sparks constructive economic evolution. Displaced retail space is increasingly being repurposed into vibrant pedestrian dining hubs, fitness studios, and artisanal craft spaces that enrich community life far better than repetitive chain stores.\n\nIn conclusion, the migration toward online retail is propelled by undeniable conveniences and economic value. By adopting biodegradable packaging and electric delivery fleets, society can fully harness the immense benefits of e-commerce while minimizing environmental impacts.",
                q2Vocab = listOf(
                    Band9Vocabulary("Seismic shift", "Collocation", "Huge fundamental change", "The migration to remote digital retail marks a seismic shift in commerce."),
                    Band9Vocabulary("Exorbitant overhead expenses", "Business Collocation", "Very high costs to run shop", "E-commerce eliminates the exorbitant overhead expenses associated with city-center leases."),
                    Band9Vocabulary("Consumer welfare", "Economic Formal Lexis", "Well-being of buyers", "Lower prices and instant home delivery directly enhance consumer welfare."),
                    Band9Vocabulary("Constructive economic evolution", "Collocation", "Positive market adaptation", "The rise of online logistics triggers constructive economic evolution in urban real estate.")
                ),
                q3Title = "Formal Letter: Preservation of Historic Municipal Building",
                q3Prompt = "The city council plans to demolish a beloved century-old public library in your neighborhood to construct a commercial shopping mall. Write a formal objection letter to the Mayor.\n\nIn your letter:\n• Explain the historical and emotional significance of the landmark\n• Point out the adverse environmental impact of unnecessary demolition\n• Propose a viable alternative for urban modernization\n(Write at least 150 words)",
                q3Letter = "Dear Mayor Sinclair,\n\nI am writing to articulate my vehement opposition to the proposed demolition of the historic Queen’s Municipal Library to facilitate the construction of a commercial shopping complex.\n\nErected in the early twentieth century, this architectural masterpiece serves as an indispensable cultural anchor for three generations of residents. Beyond its neoclassical aesthetic grandeur, the institution has provided free educational resources, quiet sanctuary, and communal literacy initiatives that no retail center can possibly replicate. Demolishing this structurally sound monument not only dishonors our civic heritage but also generates immense carbon emissions through needless masonry demolition and landfill waste.\n\nRather than bulldozing our historic core, the municipal planning board should adopt an adaptive reuse paradigm. World-class capitals routinely preserve historic façades while modernizing interior spaces into vibrant cultural hubs that house independent cafes alongside state-of-the-art digital media archives.\n\nI urgently implore you to halt demolition approvals and convene a transparent public town hall to safeguard our architectural legacy.\n\nYours sincerely,\nJulian Sterling",
                q3Vocab = listOf(
                    Band9Vocabulary("Vehement opposition", "Formal Collocation", "Intense, passionate disagreement", "Residents voiced vehement opposition against the reckless demolition plans."),
                    Band9Vocabulary("Indispensable cultural anchor", "Metaphor", "Vital pillar of tradition", "The community hall has stood for over a century as an indispensable cultural anchor."),
                    Band9Vocabulary("Adaptive reuse paradigm", "Urban Planning Term", "Repurposing old buildings", "Adopting an adaptive reuse paradigm saves millions while honoring architectural history."),
                    Band9Vocabulary("Architectural legacy", "Collocation", "Valuable heritage buildings", "A civilized city takes pride in preserving its irreplaceable architectural legacy.")
                ),
                q4Title = "Public Funding for Elite Arts vs Essential Health Services",
                q4Prompt = "Public budgets face severe economic strain. Some citizens argue that municipal governments should divert all public funding away from high-end opera houses, art galleries, and museums toward essential public healthcare and primary schooling. To what extent do you agree or disagree? (Write at least 250 words)",
                q4Essay = "The allocation of public funds during periods of macroeconomic austerity sparks fierce ideological conflict. A growing contingent of citizens asserts that taxpayer revenues should be exclusively channeled into foundational social infrastructure—specifically universal healthcare and basic primary education—rather than subsidizing high-brow artistic institutions like opera theaters and contemporary art museums. While I recognize the urgent primacy of healthcare, I firmly reject the premise that state support for creative arts should be eliminated entirely.\n\nOn the one hand, fiscal pragmatists accurately observe that public health and early childhood literacy are existential necessities without which no society can function. In times of budgetary deficits, a community struggling with overcrowded emergency wards or decrepit primary schools cannot justify lavish state subsidies for avant-garde theatrical performances that are disproportionately frequented by affluent social strata. Allocating priority capital to medical preventative care directly reduces long-term economic burdens and enhances national labor productivity.\n\nNevertheless, categorizing creative arts as dispensable indulgences reflects a myopic understanding of human well-being and economic returns. Publicly subsidized museums and art galleries are not merely repositories of elite amusement; they preserve democratic collective memory, inspire industrial design innovation, and generate billions in cultural tourism revenues. Furthermore, artistic expression provides a vital cathartic outlet that fosters psychological resilience and societal cohesion during times of collective economic stress. Starving cultural institutions of modest subsidies would impoverish the human spirit and decimate creative industries.\n\nIn conclusion, while funding for hospitals and schools must undeniably take chronological precedence in national budgets, this does not mandate the total eradication of public arts endowments. A civilized government must maintain a balanced fiscal framework that guarantees basic physical welfare while nurturing the cultural soul of its nation.",
                q4Vocab = listOf(
                    Band9Vocabulary("Existential necessities", "Formal Collocation", "Things required to survive", "Clean drinking water and primary medical access are non-negotiable existential necessities."),
                    Band9Vocabulary("Myopic understanding", "C2 Critical Lexis", "Short-sighted view", "Viewing creative arts solely through a balance-sheet lens reveals a myopic understanding."),
                    Band9Vocabulary("Vital cathartic outlet", "Psychological Lexis", "Healthy emotional release", "Music and visual storytelling offer a vital cathartic outlet for citizens enduring social distress."),
                    Band9Vocabulary("Chronological precedence", "Formal Collocation", "Priority in urgency", "Emergency healthcare funding must naturally take chronological precedence over secondary programs.")
                )
